## Checks and indicators

The script [14_check_pefa_and_indicators.py](#script-14) verifies three properties of the result.

- **Natural inputs and residuals balance exactly**, since the tool creates both sides of each of these flows.
- **Energy products balance to within the rounding of the balance.** The largest gap between supply and use of a product in any year is 3\.3 ktoe, which comes from the rounding and the statistical difference of the published balance.
- **Energy use is plausible against money purchases.** For each industry, the energy used is compared with its purchases of crude oil and gas, refined products, and electricity and gas in the supply and use tables. The ratio is an implied price in dinars per gigajoule (@fig-prices). A user who buys energy without using any, or the reverse, would signal an error of allocation; no such case occurs.

::: {.cell execution_count=8}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=8}
![Implied price of energy by industry: purchases of energy products in the supply and use tables divided by energy end use, last year (TND per GJ).](index_files/figure-typst/fig-prices-output-1.png){#fig-prices}
:::
:::


Refining (19) and electricity (35) are left out of the figure, because their purchases of energy products are mainly inputs to transformation, which the end-use figure excludes. The lowest prices appear in the miscellaneous industries, agriculture and building materials, which buy large quantities of heavy fuel, petroleum coke or natural gas at lower prices per unit of energy than diesel or electricity. Service industries show higher prices, because their energy is mostly electricity.

The script then derives the indicators of the accounts, following the Indicator sheet of the IMF tool and the key indicators of the Eurostat PEFA manual [@eurostat2014pefa, para 101]: use of natural inputs, production of energy products, transformation input, end use for energy and non-energy purposes, energy residuals, and **net domestic energy use**. This last indicator adds up the residuals supplied by each industry and by households and the energy kept in products used for non-energy purposes. It measures the energy actually used up by resident units, without the double counting of energy that is transformed before its end use [@eurostat2014pefa, paras 103 to 106]. Energy intensities relate end use to output and value added at current prices.

## Export

The script [15_export_pefa.py](#script-15) writes the accounts to three Excel workbooks, in English, French and Arabic, with the same numbers. Each holds a notes sheet, a long table of all years, the supply and use tables of each year in the layout of the PEFA sheet of the IMF tool, the same tables added up to ISIC sections, and the indicators. The workbooks can be downloaded from the Downloads page of this site.

# Results

## Supply of energy

Tunisia produces crude oil, natural gas, biomass and a growing amount of renewable electricity, and it imports a large share of its oil products and natural gas. @fig-supply shows that domestic production of energy products fell from 401 PJ in 2015 to 366 PJ in 2022, while imports rose from 317 PJ to 340 PJ. Domestic production here includes the output of power plants and of the refinery, which transform part of the primary energy.

::: {.cell execution_count=9}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=9}
![Supply of energy products by origin (PJ). Domestic production includes secondary products such as electricity and refined products.](index_files/figure-typst/fig-supply-output-1.png){#fig-supply}
:::
:::


## From supply to use

@fig-sankey follows each energy product from its origin to its use in 2022. Natural gas goes mostly into power plants, which turn it into electricity. Oil products go to households, transport, manufacturing and agriculture, and a large flow of crude oil is exported. Biofuels, mainly firewood and charcoal, are used by households. Stock changes are left out of the diagram, since a draw on stocks cannot be drawn as a flow.

::: {.cell execution_count=10}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Energy products by origin and use, last year (PJ).](index_files/figure-typst/fig-sankey-output-1.png){#fig-sankey}
:::
:::


## Energy use by industry and households

@fig-users ranks the users of energy for energy purposes in 2022. Households lead, with 132 PJ, a share that combines cooking, heating, appliances and their share of road fuel. Transport and building materials (cement, ceramics, glass) follow with about the same amount each, ahead of agriculture and the miscellaneous industries.

::: {.cell execution_count=11}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=11}
![End use of energy for energy purposes by user and product, last year (PJ). The twelve largest users.](index_files/figure-typst/fig-users-output-1.png){#fig-users}
:::
:::


## Energy intensity

Energy intensity relates the energy that an industry uses to the value it adds. Across all industries, the economy used 1\.92 TJ per million dinars of value added in 2022. @fig-intensity shows the wide range behind that average. Building materials use about 36 TJ per million dinars, several times more than transport or electricity, and service industries use much less. Because value added is measured at current prices, changes in intensity over time mix changes in efficiency with changes in prices.

::: {.cell execution_count=12}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=12}
![Energy end use per million TND of value added, by industry, last year (TJ per million TND, current prices).](index_files/figure-typst/fig-intensity-output-1.png){#fig-intensity}
:::
:::


## Net domestic energy use and the energy balance

Net domestic energy use, the residence-based headline of the accounts, can be compared with gross inland consumption, the territory-based headline of the balance (@fig-ndeu). In 2022 the first was 494 PJ and the second 487 PJ. Most of the difference comes from international bunkers (10 PJ in 2022), which the balance excludes from gross inland consumption and which the accounts, following the tool, attribute to resident carriers. A full bridge table between the two will require the residence data discussed in the methods.

::: {.cell execution_count=13}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=13}
![Net domestic energy use of the accounts and gross inland consumption of the balance (PJ).](index_files/figure-typst/fig-ndeu-output-1.png){#fig-ndeu}
:::
:::


